sum:'Capital of the Neo-Assyrian Empire under Sennacherib and Ashurbanipal; the city to which Jonah was sent.',
hist:'Sennacherib (705–681 BC) made Nineveh his capital and built the "Palace without Rival". The city fell to a coalition of Babylonians and Medes in 612 BC. Excavations by Austen Henry Layard, Hormuzd Rassam and others from the 1840s recovered palace reliefs and the Library of Ashurbanipal.',
bib:'Founded according to Genesis 10. Jonah preaches repentance here, Sennacherib returns here from his campaign against Judah, and Nahum and Zephaniah foretell its fall.',
civ:[['Early settlement',-6000,-2000],['Assyrian city',-2000,-705],['Neo-Assyrian capital',-705,-612],['Later settlement',-612,1300]],
vis:'The mounds of Kuyunjik and Nebi Yunus, restored sections of the city walls and gates (several damaged by ISIS in 2014–16 and now being restored). The Lachish reliefs and many other finds are in the British Museum.',
v:[['Genesis 10:11','Nineveh is founded.'],['Jonah 1:2','Jonah is sent to the great city of Nineveh.'],['Jonah 3:3-10','Nineveh repents at Jonah\'s preaching.'],['2 Kings 19:36-37','Sennacherib returns to Nineveh and is killed by his sons.'],['Nahum 3:7','"Nineveh is laid waste."'],['Zephaniah 2:13','The LORD will make Nineveh a desolation.'],['Matthew 12:41','The people of Nineveh repented at the preaching of Jonah.']],
