sum:'Capital of the Aramean kingdom and the city where Saul of Tarsus was converted and baptised.',
hist:'Among the oldest continuously inhabited cities. It was capital of Aram-Damascus until the Assyrian conquest of 732 BC, then part of successive empires. The Roman grid survives in the street called Straight (the decumanus maximus).',
bib:'Abraham\'s servant Eliezer is "of Damascus". Its kings war with Israel, Naaman prefers its rivers to the Jordan, and Isaiah prophesies against it. Saul is converted on the road here and escapes over the wall in a basket.',
civ:[['Aramean kingdom',-1000,-732],['Assyrian, Babylonian & Persian',-732,-332],['Hellenistic',-332,-64],['Roman & Nabataean influence',-64,395],['Byzantine',395,634],['Umayyad capital',661,750],['Later Islamic',750,2026]],
vis:'The Old City (UNESCO World Heritage), Straight Street (Souq Midhat Pasha and its continuation), the Umayyad Mosque on the site of a Roman temple of Jupiter and a church, and the traditional Chapel of Ananias.',
v:[['Genesis 15:2','Eliezer of Damascus.'],['2 Kings 5:12','Naaman: "Aren\'t Abanah and Pharpar, the rivers of Damascus, better?"'],['2 Kings 16:9','Assyria takes Damascus.'],['Isaiah 17:1','An oracle against Damascus.'],['Acts 9:1-9','Saul\'s conversion on the road to Damascus.'],['Acts 9:10-19','Ananias visits Saul in the street called Straight.'],['2 Corinthians 11:32-33','Paul is let down through a window in the wall in a basket.']],
